## Extending

### New ambisonic assets

No code changes — just drop the `.wav` in `source/data/nucleus/sounds/` (filename prefixed `NN_`), add the matching `spatial_sound_NN` Xform to `spatial_sound_locations_layer.usda`, and add the `interactions.icon_spatial_sound_NN` block to all four locales. The iconGroup template auto-discovers it on the next stage load. Drop a `NN_<name>.vtt` next to the wav (same `NN`) for optional captions.

### New ambient emitter

Two steps:

1. Add a JSON entry to `kit-app-template-main/source/data/sound_emitters.json` with the chosen `id`, `file`, and any tuning.
2. Add a matching empty `Xform` in any sublayer (or parented under an NPC) whose **leaf name equals the JSON `id`**. A dedicated `sounds_layer.usda` sublayer works well for static emitters; for dynamic emitters parent under whatever should drive their position. The Xform name is free-form — only the match between JSON `id` and prim leaf name matters.

Reload the scene. The extension logs `[sound_emitter] published N emitter(s)`. If you see `no Xform named '<id>' found under /World — skipping`, the JSON id and the Xform name don't match.

To re-trigger a publish without reopening the scene, dispatch the `soundEmitters.request` event (handy after editing the JSON).

### Adjust ambisonic rotation axes

Flip the relevant `dx` / `dy` sign in `SpatialSoundOverlay.handleVisDragMove` (and the matching device-orientation deltas in `useDeviceOrientation`). Treat `buildRotationMatrix3` as fixed — it's the canonical Three.js world-to-listener matrix and changing it almost always means re-deriving the column-major flatten too.

### Different attenuation model

`ambientSoundEngine._spawnVoice` / `_applyEmitterSettings`. All six `PannerNode` parameters (`panningModel`, `distanceModel`, `refDistance`, `maxDistance`, `rolloffFactor`, cone angles) are set in one place.

### Make ambient emitters orient-aware

`PannerNode` supports directional cones (`orientationX/Y/Z`, `coneInnerAngle`, `coneOuterAngle`). Read the emitter prim's rotation from the local-to-world matrix in `emitter_service._world_position` (split into a position+forward helper), thread it through the JSON schema (e.g. `coneInnerAngle`, `coneOuterAngle`) and the browser engine.

